Interpret a temperature change through a calibrated thermal model (@gc-calorimetry: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @general-chemistry, #source "../sources/Quimica-geral-no-laboratorio.lingua#r_48YEN2SA34DW0FPCSTMVTW3CTD", #source "../sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94", #source "../sources/Chem1-Lower.lingua#r_0NFQA43WZVMECZY6SKZJMRXWBZ", #general-chemistry "Reaction-enthalpies-and-reference-states.lingua#r_4KZGWMKS0V45XNRJ5ABNM0FWV7", #thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer "../../../physics/thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/properties/Heat-capacity-temperature-dependence-and-calorimetry.lingua#r_03R86BMA4QVQGH84GXHZZDNJKZ") { r_55B9CZ9RST5E8CANA6GZ0SNPEY
**Read:** [Química geral no laboratório — Marcela Boroski, organizer](../sources/Quimica-geral-no-laboratorio.lingua#r_48YEN2SA34DW0FPCSTMVTW3CTD), the complete thermochemistry/calorimetry activity; [Chemistry 2e — Flowers, Theopold, Langley, and Robinson](../sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94), chapter 5’s calorimetric examples and exercises; [Chem1 General Chemistry Virtual Textbook — Stephen Lower](../sources/Chem1-Lower.lingua#r_0NFQA43WZVMECZY6SKZJMRXWBZ), calorimetry and First Law applications.

A calorimeter turns observed temperature behavior into an energy estimate through a model. In a simple well-mixed approximation, heat received by solution and apparatus is q = (mc_p + C_cal)ΔT. Specify which material mass, heat capacity, apparatus contribution, and interval the model describes. Calibration, heat loss, mixing, evaporation, and the time of observation can affect the inference.

For an invented teaching case with m = 100.0 g, c_p = 4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹, C_cal = 20.0 J K⁻¹, and ΔT = 2.00 K, the estimated received heat is 876 J. If an assumed isolated reaction released this heat for an extent of 0.0100 mol under the appropriate constant-pressure conditions, the corresponding reaction enthalpy estimate is −87.6 kJ mol⁻¹. These are hypothetical inputs and conditional model results.

A temperature rise is an observation; reaction heat is inferred after accounting for other contributions. Correct a blank or heat leak through a justified model rather than subtracting whichever number improves agreement. Shared calibration contributions matter when comparing several runs. Use [Measurement and metrology](../../../../inquiry/metrology/Metrology.lingua#r_3G54ENPA974K19AB5XF0P641N5) for uncertainty and response-time analysis.

**Work:** derive the teaching result with units and independent arithmetic. Vary apparatus heat capacity, heat loss, and reaction extent separately to examine sensitivity. Read the entire laboratory activity and design a supervised calibration/comparison study, or reconstruct an authorized published dataset if practical access is unavailable.

For an advanced account compare fitted time-curve methods with a single endpoint estimate and explain which assumptions each uses. Report the energy quantity, conditions, uncertainty, and chemical interpretation together. A precise thermometer alone does not validate the thermal model.

**Continue:** [Combine reaction enthalpies with explicit states and references](Reaction-enthalpies-and-reference-states.lingua#r_4KZGWMKS0V45XNRJ5ABNM0FWV7).

